.SH SYNOPSIS
.IP
.EX
#include \(dqbashclient.h\(dq

char *bc_b64_decode(const char *encoded, size_t *out_len);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
Decodes a NUL\-terminated base64 string into raw binary data.
Accepts standard RFC 4648 base64 (\f[CR]A\-Z\f[R], \f[CR]a\-z\f[R],
\f[CR]0\-9\f[R], \f[CR]+\f[R], \f[CR]/\f[R]) with optional \f[CR]=\f[R]
padding.
.PP
The returned buffer is dynamically allocated and must be freed by the
caller using \f[CR]bc_free(3)\f[R].
The buffer is NUL\-terminated for convenience (one extra byte beyond
\f[CR]*out_len\f[R]), but the decoded data may contain embedded NUL
bytes, so \f[CR]*out_len\f[R] should be used to determine the actual
length.
.PP
This is a low\-level utility function used internally by the client
library for decoding PTY output (\f[CR]bc_pty_on_output(3)\f[R]) and
other binary payloads.
Most callers do not need to use it directly.
.SH Decoding details
.IP \(bu 2
Whitespace (spaces, tabs, newlines, carriage returns) in the input is
silently skipped, allowing formatted base64 input.
.IP \(bu 2
Missing padding is tolerated (1, 2, or 3 trailing characters without
\f[CR]=\f[R] padding are decoded correctly).
.IP \(bu 2
Invalid characters (not in the base64 alphabet, whitespace, or
\f[CR]=\f[R]) cause the function to return NULL.
.IP \(bu 2
An empty input string produces a zero\-length buffer
(\f[CR]*out_len == 0\f[R]).
