.SH NOTES
.IP \(bu 2
The output buffer size is at most \f[CR]3 * strlen(encoded) / 4\f[R]
bytes plus a NUL terminator.
.IP \(bu 2
Whitespace skipping allows decoding base64 that has been line\-wrapped
(e.g., at 76 characters as in MIME).
However, the bash\-server wire protocol never wraps base64.
.IP \(bu 2
The NUL\-terminated convenience byte means the buffer allocation is
\f[CR]*out_len + 1\f[R] bytes.
This is useful when the decoded data is known to be text, but callers
should always rely on \f[CR]*out_len\f[R] for binary data.
.IP \(bu 2
This function is not thread\-safe with respect to \f[CR]malloc\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
The implementation is constant\-time with respect to the input alphabet
validation (no early exits on invalid characters) to avoid timing side
channels, consistent with the server\-side \f[CR]b64_decode\f[R]
implementation.
